#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1762dd7a-cdff-5568-977d-6d80f2adc2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275698} {:answer/id #uuid "89802e03-4038-5079-a2d6-6fbcbfaa4f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276719} {:answer/id #uuid "8b30765e-36b9-5ec6-98f6-842bac2a8047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276741} {:answer/id #uuid "a64a6892-a119-5289-a94b-626583cc70ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276975} {:answer/id #uuid "af9e721c-402a-5c3f-9692-3951b59def5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89802e03-4038-5079-a2d6-6fbcbfaa4f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276719} :answer-field/id #uuid "b45260eb-ab24-5e89-9cc0-6905bd2dcc59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4104ec23-658c-58b7-8dbf-72086dab259b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161622" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -1+x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e7d8c11ac4b3de8473741a362ad2b78555c764da2d61f955435e8d59771cf46.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21691c25-85fe-50d4-8992-94793dce0cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275780} {:answer/id #uuid "25bc0f7d-35fe-5f7e-8ee1-1db7cf2b62c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275819} {:answer/id #uuid "52b4bc38-7d7f-5677-a106-02e57bec4368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276211} {:answer/id #uuid "8d37e41f-2bdb-5a1a-b86f-773de0e20f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276756} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc124b6-2d46-5e4f-a491-7d52913d1bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d37e41f-2bdb-5a1a-b86f-773de0e20f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276756} :answer-field/id #uuid "12ee44d9-0fea-51a9-b385-74b8a13e1ce7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35b15061-fc8b-55f3-8742-4ca105208a91" :question/math-academy-id "q-161624" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/84/8414de2e05da43c03fae3c8b1ff2102401e990188e9fd4ee59a24308ec34b086.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. As we have seen, $∂A$ consists of two concentric circles: the outer one is in $A,$ while the inner one is not.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains some of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ee6128-4b12-5d48-8dff-b37815d7c94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276067} {:answer/id #uuid "616176be-43c2-5bf1-9551-04f24aa9c7be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276357} {:answer/id #uuid "93754e25-73f5-5f71-a810-562bad5c14e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276812} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d17a5a-3c3f-5175-8d28-45025dc8c5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277199} {:answer/id #uuid "f8693685-82a4-5f06-bb61-c83ee0c7838f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1d17a5a-3c3f-5175-8d28-45025dc8c5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277199} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad7cf686-3dda-5407-8450-9e265af612dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "baee2889-195d-5ff5-b660-0a9acac1604c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161626" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∂A$\n- $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/45/4540e03fc5404474a0cd900c89004e6a5cfe7ac2d4702348e9d0395fc1c914e9.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.  \n\n![](images/2a/2a93b912f0d7b3a84bf0ec9c3b3596d080a9e71a345ff436e2f591edf042d9db.png)\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A$ or, equivalently,  $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57716a2f-adbe-53dc-9a36-7e949c3676fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276258} {:answer/id #uuid "72118cf0-8b8a-5295-919c-27f5cbbdd835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276510} {:answer/id #uuid "87dc8626-1873-5666-be98-d2e39efb8926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276704} {:answer/id #uuid "a38f3455-f66a-5215-a435-be8ebbaf60e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276950} {:answer/id #uuid "bc63d771-387a-52c7-99fd-876c0770a2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57716a2f-adbe-53dc-9a36-7e949c3676fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276258} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bbeb67-b26e-5d94-986d-584288de76a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32d0249e-396e-5abf-9c3e-a9a01870f500" :question/math-academy-id "q-161643" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4y>{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a380742f47d97062d56b482592cd2d2961dcc40262131a99886b815c507ab6e3.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/26d264d702368ed6fe05577d7c34630bd16b41bdba8d206dd4db39763db02938.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊈A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a444db7-d5d6-57e6-8e48-407211cee868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275723} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1bbcf0-1dcb-5285-af5d-b33113f92715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275745} {:answer/id #uuid "3316cb25-b494-5e84-8c8c-14602033695e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275932} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c15056-e18f-5bda-8f30-b2945d8e51a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277103} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0c797b-7971-52bb-a227-fbf910934ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6c15056-e18f-5bda-8f30-b2945d8e51a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277103} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1c97ad8-2e05-54f3-9147-887f2a79b763" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "91207617-bfa9-5efa-a562-b7d640b0c541" :question/math-academy-id "q-161651" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $A=∂A\\cup \\text{int}A$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a39ecae41a88e873fa9eecb30a5b554be9ec98cf630851715ab35f2f873710f2.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements I and III are false. Since ${C}_{1}⊂∂A-A,$ we must have that $∂A⊄A$ and also that $A\\ne ∂A\\cup \\text{int}A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052b75ab-358d-5073-b438-8aabbcf16598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278638} {:answer/id #uuid "21f1b1b1-4cbf-5384-abf1-011fb4b8e732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278897} {:answer/id #uuid "62d28ecd-0906-545c-93e9-7da6ccef73dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279488} {:answer/id #uuid "84b65459-6cd3-563c-a465-2e40b3ffdf57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279785} {:answer/id #uuid "97292980-0d47-5775-9ecb-d399051901ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97292980-0d47-5775-9ecb-d399051901ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279929} :answer-field/id #uuid "f48d194b-9d36-54e9-be97-ae912aef2d30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46773dfb-2950-5619-a7ba-c3559b8d83bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161652" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. $∂A\\cap A\\ne ∅$\n\nII. $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n\nIII. $A=\\text{int}\\,A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/22/2273cd843c4bfaeaff10d49d3400d7661262673639a66034638d805eef5b1e47.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have  $∂A\\cap A=∅.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III  only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b24e418-85ae-5620-871f-6fadf0d095e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278697} {:answer/id #uuid "29c92160-c1eb-56ed-9791-f6e1013149b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278961} {:answer/id #uuid "8253b24f-d960-5879-a212-1945f39d7682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279759} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec6c6ee-706a-5375-90e8-ede072b04c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279862} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d68eed-e257-52f7-9b26-4c25a946c054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29c92160-c1eb-56ed-9791-f6e1013149b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278961} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a894510-e33f-5f5d-8250-b299a0d3e001" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b9d32a6-dbbc-54ba-9b9d-46f810008dd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161845" :question/problem "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- The function $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n- The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is also true. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01683696-1768-510b-b2ef-229cf3c6ce7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278595} {:answer/id #uuid "21a3a624-267c-5525-b0d6-fa9087ace471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278895} {:answer/id #uuid "578cbb0d-4b00-595c-8c1f-db8ea0aabe49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279395} {:answer/id #uuid "678ad038-6468-51ae-9b8c-30158d54f8f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279530} {:answer/id #uuid "d1bbf243-f303-5b79-9edf-c8d2849b004f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "678ad038-6468-51ae-9b8c-30158d54f8f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279530} :answer-field/id #uuid "e39dcd4a-7825-5c92-92a2-098ef045e0c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46fbce01-7db5-5630-8364-44d4a151bbf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162346" :question/problem "Let $z$ and $w$ be $4$th roots of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${w}^{4}+1=1$\n- ${(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4}=1$\n- $\\frac{z}{w}$ is a $4$th root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If $z$ and $w$ are $4$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{4}={w}^{4}=1.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n{w}^{4}+1=1+1=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4} & =\\frac{{z}^{4}}{{w}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement II, we have that ${(\\frac{z}{w})}^{4}=1,$ which means that $\\frac{z}{w}$ is a $4$th root of unity. \n\nIn general, the quotient of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "159fcf09-f3bf-5eae-8ee5-70cac24cfdff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278785} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2aa89f-0fb1-552e-bcf1-e5d22bff96c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279107} {:answer/id #uuid "48982c83-3829-5a01-a1b7-8bff71aa1b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279239} {:answer/id #uuid "89a66cea-cc4e-5d42-b1bd-86ad16a6ebd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279817} {:answer/id #uuid "c9733f31-89dd-59b5-bc59-6b88a5d62b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9733f31-89dd-59b5-bc59-6b88a5d62b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280355} :answer-field/id #uuid "7db6fc7b-b924-5f9c-9c1d-950c074bfbb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b77c81df-773f-534f-b1e3-2174eb2531c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162347" :question/problem "Let $z$ be a $6$th root of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $({z}^{3})^{2}=-1$\n- ${z}^{3}$ is a $6$th root of unity\n- ${z}^{3}$ is a square root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({z}^{3})^{2}={z}^{6}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({z}^{3})^{6} & =({z}^{6})^{3} \\\\  & =(1)^{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that (by definition) ${z}^{3}$ is a $6$th root of unity.\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement I, we have that \n\n$$\n({z}^{3})^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nThis means that (by definition) ${z}^{3}$ is a square root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066100ae-d403-5565-b092-18ed907cf446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278650} {:answer/id #uuid "3a21f67b-04e9-51b6-b2f2-87910d42dba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279106} {:answer/id #uuid "8a168bae-f461-5782-acce-733c8d30db6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279824} {:answer/id #uuid "9c4173bf-d317-5b23-9d1c-9ee28afe9ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279979} {:answer/id #uuid "f50fc075-cdb1-5948-b267-cdca2c9b236f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "066100ae-d403-5565-b092-18ed907cf446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278650} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fde1e26-7b8a-5610-85f5-454d3ec9a8f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b299b05-4c26-5412-a581-f913a340d791" :question/math-academy-id "q-162348" :question/problem "Let $z$ and $w$ be $8$th roots of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${z}^{8}=1$\n- $(zw)^{8}=1$\n- $zw$ is an $8$th root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $z$ and $w$ are $8$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{8}={w}^{8}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(zw)^{8} & ={z}^{8}{w}^{8} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement II, we have that $(zw)^{8}=1,$ which means that $zw$ is an $8$th root of unity. \n\nIn general, the product of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0562b352-4bde-5c23-b5a1-ae34aaafb142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278640} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3e4451-82d0-5454-b503-9a3725bcfabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279553} {:answer/id #uuid "a7453063-397e-5c14-a2f3-c8d6f10fdf34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280073} {:answer/id #uuid "bdef9375-0239-53a2-bd45-8e2529f94cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280260} {:answer/id #uuid "d22bd3b9-17de-5f4d-baf8-343dd0530797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a3e4451-82d0-5454-b503-9a3725bcfabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279553} :answer-field/id #uuid "e69f0859-b3c4-5c51-a52a-95afdd5e344f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4a65d62e-3083-571b-b429-c51c7b117795" :question/math-academy-id "q-162349" :question/problem "Let $z$ be an $8$th root of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $({z}^{4})^{2}=-1$\n- ${z}^{4}$ is an $8$th root of unity\n- ${z}^{4}$ is a square root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({z}^{4})}^{2}={z}^{8}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({z}^{4})^{8} & =({z}^{8})^{4} \\\\  & =(1)^{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that (by definition) ${z}^{4}$ is a $8$th root of unity.\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement I, we have that \n\n$$\n({z}^{4})^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nThis means that (by definition) ${z}^{4}$ is a square root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16feb184-d357-53c7-9778-5e97cf1ca335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278796} {:answer/id #uuid "5fee0df5-f222-5b38-a65b-436a15c274bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279470} {:answer/id #uuid "6fcee9e6-8b9c-53ad-988c-edb5886dbf75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279607} {:answer/id #uuid "c412b129-79de-5309-8d14-fb3c7439cc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280317} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc4a36e-3a93-5957-9b39-9f8653de46ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fcee9e6-8b9c-53ad-988c-edb5886dbf75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279607} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4c8f7f-28c0-58d5-8cf8-12c5e21d0394" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec73ee18-5d78-5b94-8905-bcb7e7e3a771" :question/math-academy-id "q-162350" :question/problem "Let $z$ and $w$ be $4$th roots of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${z}^{4}=-1$\n- $(zw)^{4}=0$\n- $zw$ is a $4$th root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If $z$ and $w$ are $4$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{4}={w}^{4}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(zw)^{4} & ={z}^{4}{w}^{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have seen above that $(zw)^{4}=1,$ which means that $zw$ is a $4$th root of unity. \n\nIn general, the product of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007e3d6a-4d9f-5068-9677-962c469778a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278589} {:answer/id #uuid "a71ddf06-c1d1-599f-8807-654a1bc2a38a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280070} {:answer/id #uuid "c017cd9c-fa9b-584a-a3e5-167b21b4d322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280283} {:answer/id #uuid "ef044fb1-8503-582f-808e-4db0ada40bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280686} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d312db-c3d1-5a33-ac03-8991872badaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef044fb1-8503-582f-808e-4db0ada40bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280686} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b19619f-e593-5557-99d9-f07302fd0b39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6154f21e-3dc4-5e29-9ac5-5bb6e4c1043c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162351" :question/problem "Let $z$ and $w$ be cube roots of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${z}^{3}=1$\n- $(z+z)^{3}=8$\n- $w+w=2w$ is a cube root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $z$ and $w$ are cube roots of unity, then (by definition) we have that \n\n$$\n{z}^{3}={w}^{3}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. We obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+z)^{3} & =(2z)^{3} \\\\  & ={2}^{3}\\cdot {z}^{3} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. From statement II, we have that $(w+w)^{3}=8\\ne 1,$ which means that $w+w=2w$ is *not* a cube root of unity. \n\nIn general, the sum of $n$th roots of unity is *not* an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e097dc-5cbc-5475-a347-f736fa0a0085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279064} {:answer/id #uuid "627e72ad-78d6-5cd0-92a1-f250ec9e6398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279487} {:answer/id #uuid "77023edc-8fd5-533e-8f0f-06f6de498f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279654} {:answer/id #uuid "9675a4e0-e229-53aa-b0a5-f65cc721c6ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279924} {:answer/id #uuid "deabc480-dd74-54b7-b0a6-0681f65b4aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35e097dc-5cbc-5475-a347-f736fa0a0085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279064} :answer-field/id #uuid "dddb2faf-6904-5f9d-83dc-ccae19db486b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10662523-d7b1-54f8-907f-f332605eaff8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162368" :question/problem "Let $z$ be a $4$th root of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${z}^{4}=-1$\n- $(z+z)^{4}=16$\n- $z+z=2z$  is not a $4$th root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If $z$ is a  $4$th root of unity, then (by definition) we have that\n\n$$\n{z}^{4}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+z)^{4} & =(2z)^{4} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}\\cdot {z}^{4} \\\\  & =16\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. From statement II, we have that $(z+z)^{4}\\ne 1,$ which means that $z+z=2z$ is *not* a $4$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e42234c3-4dbf-5b79-a5ff-11e228be4469" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c51958-8ef4-52c7-b869-046cef0ac581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278618} {:answer/id #uuid "8971b69c-7d3f-5ca3-a1b5-940a8d208ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279815} {:answer/id #uuid "a17a122f-18bb-5811-9a74-7caf81094891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280027} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ae3ed5-1e5a-5b4e-be5c-c40bf41bbf20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280313} {:answer/id #uuid "f629aa2d-8006-58cb-9244-a7c7bf8bf97d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a17a122f-18bb-5811-9a74-7caf81094891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280027} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d02ff57-cf82-5c69-b72f-570a5d874496" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b7bc6df-b3cb-50da-83fc-955ab52a05f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162369" :question/problem "Let $z$ and $w$ be $6$th roots of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $z{w}^{6}=w$\n- $(zw)^{6}=1$\n- $zw$ is not a $6$th root of unity" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,  since $z$ and $w$ are $6$th roots of unity, then (by definition) we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{6}={w}^{6}=1.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\nz{w}^{6}=z\\cdot 1=z\\ne w.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(zw)^{6} & ={z}^{6}{w}^{6} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have seen above that $(zw)^{6}=1,$ which means that $zw$ is a $6$th root of unity.  \n\nIn general, the product of $n$th roots of unity is an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II  only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03fae4e2-b796-5141-b87c-f1b4312776c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278623} {:answer/id #uuid "560257e8-1ca3-572c-91a0-bf9c4c6479ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279377} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f53f36-9565-5df6-a9bf-bbb66fe548d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280032} {:answer/id #uuid "c134a8e8-ec53-5f35-9c7d-c9248aaa4b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280288} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c1f42d-1d9e-5704-971e-9225dd7bfa1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03fae4e2-b796-5141-b87c-f1b4312776c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278623} :answer-field/id #uuid "f35c9888-1ae6-5550-8edd-912098edf6cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d62a3796-3a93-54ec-8c55-9e54ca670215" :question/math-academy-id "q-162379" :question/problem "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(x)\\in N,$ where $p(x)={x}^{3}-1.$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in N$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in N.$\n- The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have, $p(x)={x}^{3}-1\\notin N$ because\n\n$$\np(0)=(0)^{3}-1=-1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x),g(x)\\in N,$ where $f(0)=0$ and $g(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=0+0=0.\n$$\n\nHence $f(x)+g(x)\\in N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
